1. Nf3 {Keymer Variation move order. White waits for Black's setup instead of committing to a normal Queen's Gambit.} d5 2. e3 {The point is practical: after ...Nf6 White will play c4, but starting 2.c4 immediately allows ...d4.} Nf6  (2... c5  3. b3 {Against the principled early ...c5, switch to b3 first.} Nc6  (3... Nf6  4. Bb2 e6 5. d4 cxd4 6. exd4 Bb4+ 7. c3 Be7 8. Bd3 Nc6 9. O-O b6 10. Nbd2 Bb7 11. Re1 Rc8 12. Nf1 O-O 13. Ng3 Re8 14. Qd2 Bf8 15. h4 {Keymer-Kuzubov model game theme: h4-h5, Ne5, and steady kingside pressure.} Qc7 16. h5 h6 17. Ne5 Nxe5 18. dxe5 Nd7 19. Qf4) 4. Bb2 Nf6 5. Bb5 {Leon gives Bb5 against the ...Nc6/...Nf6 setup. If Black replies with ...e6 too casually, Bxc6 can leave a pleasant reverse-Nimzo structure.} Bd7  (5... e6?!  6. Bxc6+ bxc6 7. d3 Bd6 8. Nbd2 O-O 9. e4 {White's pieces are easy to place; Black's c8 bishop can be a long-term problem.}) 6. Bxc6 Bxc6 7. Ne5 Rc8 8. O-O e6 9. f4 {A practical setup: simple development plus e4/f4 ideas.})  (2... g6  3. d4 {Against Slav-with-g6 setups, Leon's easy recommendation is d4. The g7 bishop often has little bite against this centre.} Nf6 4. c4 c6 5. Nc3 Bg7 6. Be2 O-O 7. O-O)  (2... a6  3. d4 {A6 before development is not scary; Leon prefers d4 rather than memorising too much theory.} Nf6 4. c4 e6 5. Nc3) 3. c4 {Strike the centre only after ...Nf6. Leon divides the repertoire mainly into c6 systems, e6 systems, and c5 systems.} c6  (3... e6  4. Nc3 {Prefer Nc3 here too; b3 is possible, but Nc3 keeps move-order control.} Be7  (4... c5  5. cxd5 {Determine the central structure before playing d4.} exd5  (5... Nxd5  6. d4 {Semi-Tarrasch structure. If Black gives up the centre with a piece recapture, White can consider Qc2/Qb1 plus kingside expansion.} cxd4 7. exd4 Be7 8. Bd3 Nc6 9. O-O O-O 10. Re1 {Ideas: Qc2, a3 to stop ...Nb4, and sometimes h4.}) 6. d4 {Tarrasch/IQP structure. White's goal is slow pressure, not a forced attack.} Nc6 7. Be2 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bd6 9. O-O O-O 10. b3)  (4... b6  5. cxd5 exd5 6. d4 Bb7 7. Bd3 Nbd7 8. O-O Bd6 9. Ne2 O-O 10. Ng3 {Queen's Indian style. White gets a playable structure and reroutes the knight rather than rushing kingside action.})  (4... dxc4  5. Bxc4 {Queen's Gambit Accepted branch. The video did not dwell here; use normal development and keep the Keymer flexibility.} c5 6. O-O Be7 7. Qe2 O-O 8. Rd1) 5. b3 O-O 6. Bb2 b6  (6... c5  7. cxd5 exd5 8. d4 {Do not play Qc2 into this structure. Forget the attack and play an IQP game against d5: castle, Rc1, and use dark-square manoeuvres such as Na4-d4-c5.} Nc6 9. Be2 cxd4 10. Nxd4) 7. cxd5 {After ...b6, Leon says White should take on d5 almost all the time.} exd5 8. d4 {This structure is comfortable because ...b6 makes it harder for Black to support d5 cleanly with ...c6, and later ...c5 creates hanging-pawn targets.} Bb7 9. Bd3 {Put the bishop on d3, not e2, so Ne2-g3 remains available.} Nbd7 10. O-O Re8 11. Ne2 Bd6 12. Ng3 {Recurring Keymer/Mendonca manoeuvre.}) 4. Nc3 {Important move order. Play Nc3 before b3 so early ...Bf5 or ...Bg4 can be punished.} e6  (4... Bf5?!  5. cxd5 cxd5 6. Qb3 {Typical punishment: attack b7 before Black has time for ...e6.} Qb6 7. Qxb6 axb6 8. d4 {White accepts a favorable Exchange Slav type structure with extra tempi.})  (4... Bg4?!  5. cxd5 cxd5 6. Qb3 Bxf3 7. Qxb7)  (4... Nbd7  5. b3 e6 6. Bb2 Bd6 7. Qc2 {This transposes to the Semi-Slav themes: Qc2 against ...e5, or Rg1/g4 if Black castles and plays ...b6.})  (4... a6  5. b3 {Leon says a6 can be met by either d4 or this playable b3 sideline.} Bf5 6. Ba3 {Nihal Sarin-Stefanova model idea: ask questions about Black's developed bishop and keep a small edge.}) 5. b3 {Do not transpose to main-line Semi-Slav with d4. White develops around the centre.} Nbd7 6. Bb2 Bd6 7. Qc2 {Chosen so ...e5 can be met by cxd5 and Nb5.} O-O 8. Be2 b6  (8... e5  9. cxd5 cxd5 10. Nb5 Bb8 11. Rc1 {Switch plans. White is no longer just attacking the king; he plays for Nb5-c7 ideas, the bishop pair, and dark-square pressure.})  (8... a6  9. d4 {If ...a6 is designed to prepare ...e5 while stopping Nb5, Leon recommends changing plans and claiming a small central edge.} Re8 10. O-O)  (8... Re8  9. Rg1 b6 10. g4 Bb7 11. g5 Ne4 12. Nxe4 dxe4 13. Ne5 {A representative attacking structure. Leon's lesson is that Black's human defence is difficult while White's play is simple.}) 9. Rg1 {The key attacking signal. Leon says this scores well and was used by Keymer; White does not need to castle long immediately.} Bb7 10. g4 {Plan: g4, g5, h4, h5, and only then consider castling long if it is useful.} *

